# AI on the phone

## Overview

This notebook runs machine-learning models on the phone's CPU, with no GPU
or cloud service: object detection on a camera photo, speech to text, and a
chat with a small language model. First it looks at how slowly plain Elixir
tensors run on it. It builds on the earlier notebooks: Wi-Fi (01), the
camera (05) and audio (10).

## The stack

The AI stack has three kinds of parts:

* **Capability packages** do the work, each a native library wrapped for
  the BEAM: [`llama_cpp_ex`](https://hexdocs.pm/llama_cpp_ex) runs language
  models (llama.cpp), [`whisper_cpp`](https://hexdocs.pm/whisper_cpp)
  transcribes speech (whisper.cpp, with Silero voice detection) and
  [`evision`](https://hexdocs.pm/evision) runs the YOLO object detector
  (OpenCV).
* **`nerves_ai`** is the composer. It puts one small facade in front of each
  capability (`NervesAI.LLM`, `NervesAI.STT`, `NervesAI.Vision`), downloads
  model files with checksums (`NervesAI.Models`), reads and writes WAV files
  (`NervesAI.Audio.WAV`).
* **`nerves_data_resize`** grows the data partition on first boot so models
  fit.

Nothing is configured to start at boot. This notebook downloads each model
when you need it and starts the matching capability with `Kino.start_child/1`.

The Snapdragon 632 has two clusters of four Kryo 250 cores: `cpu0-3` are
the slower "Silver" cores and `cpu4-7` the faster "Gold" cores. Neither
cluster has the dot-product or half-precision instructions that newer ARM
chips use to speed up model maths, so the native libraries are built for
plain ARMv8.0. The engines use four threads each.

Two things were measured and dropped because they made no measurable
difference on this phone: a "run fast" lease holding the Gold cluster at
full speed around a burst (`schedutil` already ramps the clock within
milliseconds), and pinning the VM's threads to the Gold cluster (the
kernel already schedules this CPU-bound work onto the Gold cores on its
own — alternating pinned and unpinned runs of the pipelines below showed
no timing difference).

## Tensors on the BEAM

[Nx](https://hexdocs.pm/nx) is Elixir's tensor library. This firmware sets
[`NxEigen.Backend`](https://hexdocs.pm/nx_eigen) as its default backend
(`config :nx, default_backend: NxEigen.Backend`): the Eigen C++ library with
NEON, built for this CPU, rather than Nx's plain Elixir backend, which is
far too slow on a phone. The object detector below does its image work in
OpenCV; Nx is there for your own tensor code and for libraries such as
`nx_image` and `nx_signal`.

## Models: downloaded when you need them

The next steps use trained models. They're too big for the firmware and
none is downloaded automatically: each step fetches its model the first
time with `NervesAI.Models.ensure_one/3`. It checks the SHA-256, writes the
file atomically to `/data/models` and skips the download when the file is
already there.

The phone needs internet access for the first run. If this doesn't say
`:internet`, connect to Wi-Fi with the Wi-Fi notebook:

```elixir
VintageNet.get(["connection"])
```

These are two models known to run on this phone, with their checksums:

```elixir
catalog = %{
  tinyllama: [
    source:
      {:hf, "TheBloke/TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0-GGUF", "tinyllama-1.1b-chat-v1.0.Q4_K_M.gguf"},
    path: "/data/models/tinyllama.gguf",
    sha256: "9fecc3b3cd76bba89d504f29b616eedf7da85b96540e490ca5824d3f7d2776a0"
  ],
  yolov5n: [
    source: {:url, "https://github.com/ultralytics/yolov5/releases/download/v7.0/yolov5n.onnx"},
    path: "/data/models/yolov5n.onnx",
    sha256: "04f0e55c26f58d17145b36045780fe1250d5bd2187543e11568e5141d05b3262"
  ]
}

Map.keys(catalog)
```

Two helpers for the steps below: `fetch` downloads a model (printing a
heartbeat so a multi-minute download on slow Wi-Fi doesn't look hung) and
returns its path, and `start` starts a capability under this notebook's
supervisor and waits for its model to load.

```elixir
fetch = fn id, spec ->
  heartbeat =
    Task.async(fn ->
      Stream.interval(5_000)
      |> Stream.each(fn n -> IO.puts("#{id}: still downloading... (#{(n + 1) * 5} s)") end)
      |> Stream.run()
    end)

  result = NervesAI.Models.ensure_one(id, spec)
  Task.shutdown(heartbeat, :brutal_kill)

  case result do
    {:ok, path} ->
      path

    {:error, reason} ->
      raise "Couldn't download #{id}: #{inspect(reason)}. " <>
              "Is the phone online? Check with the Wi-Fi notebook."
  end
end

start = fn adapter, config ->
  case Kino.start_child(adapter.child_spec(config)) do
    {:ok, _pid} -> :ok
    {:error, {:already_started, _pid}} -> :ok
    {:error, reason} -> raise "Couldn't start #{inspect(adapter)}: #{inspect(reason)}"
  end

  ready =
    Enum.any?(1..240, fn _ ->
      adapter.ready?(config) or (Process.sleep(500) && false)
    end)

  if ready, do: :ready, else: raise("#{inspect(adapter)} didn't load its model; see the log")
end
```

## Find objects in a photo (YOLO)

**YOLOv5n** finds and labels objects in a picture: people, chairs,
bottles, laptops and 76 other everyday categories (the COCO dataset;
`NervesAI.Vision.COCO.labels/0` lists them). The model is 4 MB.
`NervesAI.Vision.Evision` runs it with OpenCV and `NervesAI.Vision` is the
interface to call.

```elixir
yolo_path = fetch.(:yolov5n, catalog.yolov5n)

start.(NervesAI.Vision.Evision, path: yolo_path)
```

### Take a photo and detect

Point the back of the phone at something and run the cell. It takes a
2×2-binned photo (quicker than full resolution) and lists what the model
found. Each box is `[x1, y1, x2, y2]` in pixels of the photo.

```elixir
photo = "/tmp/detect.jpg"
IO.puts("Taking the photo...")
{:ok, _} = Fp3Camera.snap(:rear, photo, binned: true)
IO.puts("Looking for objects...")

detections = NervesAI.Vision.detect(NervesAI.Vision, photo)

for d <- detections do
  %{object: d.label, confidence: Float.round(d.score, 2), box: inspect(Enum.map(d.box, &round/1))}
end
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

`detect/3` takes `conf:` (minimum score, default 0.25) and `iou:` options.
This cell draws the boxes as an SVG overlay on the photo.

### See it

```elixir
{height, width, _channels} = Evision.imread(photo).shape

boxes =
  for %{box: [x1, y1, x2, y2], label: label, score: score} <- detections do
    """
    <rect x="#{x1}" y="#{y1}" width="#{x2 - x1}" height="#{y2 - y1}"
          fill="none" stroke="yellow" stroke-width="#{width / 250}"/>
    <text x="#{x1 + 6}" y="#{y1 + width / 30}" fill="yellow" font-size="#{width / 32}"
          font-family="sans-serif">#{label} #{round(score * 100)}%</text>
    """
  end

jpeg = Base.encode64(File.read!(photo))

Kino.HTML.new("""
<svg viewBox="0 0 #{width} #{height}" style="max-width: 100%">
  <image href="data:image/jpeg;base64,#{jpeg}" width="#{width}" height="#{height}"/>
  #{Enum.join(boxes)}
</svg>
""")
```

### Live detection

The same model on live video. The camera ISP hands over 640×480 frames
at 30 fps, and each one is shown with the latest boxes drawn on it, so
the video stays real time. Detection takes about 300 ms, so it runs on
its own beside the video, always on the newest frame, and the boxes
jump a few times a second to keep up. OpenCV gets 4 of the 8 cores for
it and the video keeps the others.

Press **Stop** to end it.

```elixir
camera = :rear
video = Kino.Frame.new() |> Kino.render()
stop = Kino.Control.button("Stop") |> Kino.render()
Kino.Control.subscribe(stop, :stop)

Evision.setNumThreads(4)

# Asks for a frame whenever it's free, and sends back what it found in it
parent = self()

detector =
  spawn_link(fn ->
    detect = fn detect ->
      send(parent, {:free, self()})

      receive do
        {:frame, bgr} ->
          send(parent, {:detections, NervesAI.Vision.detect(NervesAI.Vision, bgr)})
          detect.(detect)

        :stop ->
          :ok
      end
    end

    detect.(detect)
  end)

draw = fn bgr, detections ->
  Enum.reduce(detections, bgr, fn %{box: [x1, y1, x2, y2], label: label, score: score}, img ->
    img
    |> Evision.rectangle({round(x1), round(y1)}, {round(x2), round(y2)}, {0, 255, 255},
      thickness: 2
    )
    |> Evision.putText("#{label} #{round(score * 100)}%", {round(x1) + 4, round(y1) + 16},
      Evision.Constant.cv_FONT_HERSHEY_SIMPLEX(), 0.5, {0, 255, 255}
    )
  end)
end

{:ok, feed} = Fp3Camera.subscribe(camera, width: 640, height: 480)

show = fn show, detections, free ->
  receive do
    {:stop, _event} ->
      :stopped

    {:detections, detections} ->
      show.(show, detections, free)

    {:free, detector} ->
      show.(show, detections, detector)

    {:camera_frame, %{data: nv12, width: w, height: h}} ->
      bgr =
        nv12
        |> Evision.Mat.from_binary({:u, 8}, div(h * 3, 2), w, 1)
        |> Evision.cvtColor(Evision.Constant.cv_COLOR_YUV2BGR_NV12())

      if free, do: send(free, {:frame, bgr})

      jpeg =
        Evision.imencode(".jpg", draw.(bgr, detections),
          params: [Evision.Constant.cv_IMWRITE_JPEG_QUALITY(), 70]
        )

      Kino.Frame.render(video, Kino.Image.new(jpeg, :jpeg))
      show.(show, detections, nil)
  end
end

show.(show, [], nil)
Fp3Camera.unsubscribe(feed)
send(detector, :stop)
```

## Speech to text (Whisper)

**Whisper** turns speech into text. This is OpenAI's `tiny.en` model
(English only, about 75 MB) in the format whisper.cpp reads, run by
`NervesAI.STT.WhisperCpp`. A second, tiny model (about 0.9 MB), **Silero**,
detects where speech is so silence is skipped before Whisper sees it.

```elixir
whisper_path =
  fetch.(:whisper,
    source: {:hf, "ggerganov/whisper.cpp", "ggml-tiny.en.bin"},
    path: "/data/models/ggml-tiny.en.bin",
    sha256: "921e4cf8686fdd993dcd081a5da5b6c365bfde1162e72b08d75ac75289920b1f"
  )

vad_path =
  fetch.(:silero,
    source: {:hf, "ggml-org/whisper-vad", "ggml-silero-v5.1.2.bin"},
    path: "/data/models/ggml-silero-v5.1.2.bin",
    sha256: "29940d98d42b91fbd05ce489f3ecf7c72f0a42f027e4875919a28fb4c04ea2cf"
  )

start.(NervesAI.STT.WhisperCpp,
  path: whisper_path,
  vad_path: vad_path,
  language: "en"
)
```

### Transcribe a recording

The firmware ships an 11-second excerpt of John F. Kennedy's 1961
inaugural address. Nothing here converts sample rates, so the audio has to
be 16 kHz; this file is.

```elixir
sample = Application.app_dir(:nerves_livebook_fp3, "priv/audio/jfk.wav")
IO.puts("Transcribing...")
{:ok, %{text: text, segments: segments}} = NervesAI.STT.transcribe(NervesAI.STT, {:wav, sample})

IO.puts(text)
Kino.DataTable.new(segments)
```

### Transcribe yourself

Records 5 seconds from the phone's main microphone. Speak (in English)
while it runs. The microphone needs a system image whose kernel drives the
WCD9335 audio codec; without it, `arecord` fails here.

The recording is asked for at 16 kHz mono, and ALSA's `plughw` device
converts from the microphone's rate. **This has not been tested on the
device.** If `arecord` rejects `-r 16000`, record at 48 kHz as the audio
notebook (10) does; `NervesAI.STT.transcribe/3` then answers
`{:error, {:unsupported_rate, 48000}}` and the recording needs a resampling
step first.

```elixir
microphone = [
  {"MultiMedia2 Mixer SLIMBUS_0_TX", :on},
  {"SLIM TX7 MUX", "DEC7"},
  {"ADC MUX7", "DMIC"},
  {"DMIC MUX7", "DMIC1"},
  {"AIF1_CAP Mixer SLIM TX7", :on}
]

for {name, value} <- microphone, do: {name, ExAudio.cset(name, value)}

arecord = fn seconds, path ->
  System.cmd("arecord", ~w(-D plughw:0,1 -f S16_LE -r 16000 -c 1 -d #{seconds} #{path}),
    stderr_to_stdout: true
  )
end

# The first capture after boot returns silence; a short one wakes the path up.
arecord.(1, "/tmp/warmup.wav")
IO.puts("Recording for 5 seconds: speak now!")
{output, status} = arecord.(5, "/tmp/me.wav")
if status != 0, do: IO.puts(output)

IO.puts("Transcribing...")
NervesAI.STT.transcribe(NervesAI.STT, {:wav, "/tmp/me.wav"})
```

## Chat with a language model (TinyLlama)

**TinyLlama 1.1B Chat** is a small language model: about 670 MB, the
biggest step. `NervesAI.LLM.LlamaCpp` runs it with llama.cpp. The model
file carries its own chat template, so you pass the conversation as a list
of messages and the library formats the prompt the way TinyLlama was
trained.

```elixir
llm_path = fetch.(:tinyllama, catalog.tinyllama)

start.(NervesAI.LLM.LlamaCpp, path: llm_path, n_ctx: 1024)
```

### Ask a question

Change `question` and run the cell again. The answer is printed piece by
piece as the model writes it. `max_tokens` caps its length.

```elixir
question = "What three things should I see in Brussels?"

messages = [
  %{role: "system", content: "You are a helpful, concise travel guide."},
  %{role: "user", content: question}
]

started = System.monotonic_time(:millisecond)

pieces =
  NervesAI.LLM.stream_chat(NervesAI.LLM, messages, max_tokens: 128, temp: 0.7)
  |> Enum.reduce(0, fn
    text, count when is_binary(text) ->
      IO.write(text)
      count + 1

    {:error, reason}, count ->
      IO.puts("\n[generation failed: #{inspect(reason)}]")
      count
  end)

seconds = (System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) - started) / 1000
IO.puts("\n\n#{pieces} pieces in #{Float.round(seconds, 1)} s")
```

`NervesAI.LLM.chat/3` takes the same arguments and returns the whole answer
at once.

## Already on this phone

The models downloaded so far (sizes in MB):

```elixir
case File.ls("/data/models") do
  {:ok, files} ->
    for f <- Enum.sort(files), do: {f, div(File.stat!(Path.join("/data/models", f)).size, 1_000_000)}

  {:error, :enoent} ->
    []
end
```
